# UI Accessibility & Testability

Every interactive element must be accessible to assistive technologies and locatable by Playwright tests. Follow the W3C WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices.

## First rule of ARIA: prefer native HTML

Use semantic HTML elements instead of ARIA roles whenever possible:

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `<div role="button" tabIndex={0}>` | `<button>` |
| `<div role="navigation">` | `<nav>` |
| `<span role="link">` | `<a href="...">` |
| `<div role="textbox">` | `<input>` or `<textarea>` |

Native elements already have built-in roles, keyboard behavior, and focus management. Adding ARIA to a `<div>` forces you to reimplement all of that manually.

## Labeling — W3C priority order

Use the **highest applicable method**. Lower methods override higher ones, so don't double up unnecessarily:

### 1. Visible `<Label htmlFor>` (preferred for form fields)

```tsx
<Label htmlFor="email">Email</Label>
<Input id="email" type="email" />
```

Screen readers announce "Email, edit text". No `aria-label` needed — the `<label>` association is sufficient.

### 2. `aria-labelledby` (references another visible element)

```tsx
<h2 id="section-title">Workshop Details</h2>
<form aria-labelledby="section-title">...</form>
```

Use when a visible heading or text already describes the element. Avoids duplicating text.

### 3. `aria-label` (only when no visible text exists)

Use `aria-label` ONLY for elements that have **no visible label or text content**:

| Use case | Example |
|---|---|
| Icon-only button | `<button aria-label="Close dialog"><X /></button>` |
| Toggle button | `<button aria-label={visible ? "Hide password" : "Show password"}>` |
| Landmark disambiguation | `<nav aria-label="Main navigation">` vs `<nav aria-label="Footer links">` |
| Search input without visible label | `<Input aria-label="Search workshops" />` |

### When NOT to use `aria-label`

- Input already has `<Label htmlFor>` — the label is sufficient
- Button already has descriptive text — `<button>Sign In</button>` is self-describing
- Link already has descriptive text — `<a href="/workshops">View workshops</a>` is self-describing

`aria-label` **overrides** all other naming. If an input has both `<Label>` and `aria-label`, screen readers will ONLY announce the `aria-label`, ignoring the visible label. This creates a disconnect between what sighted and non-sighted users perceive.

## Error and validation states

Form fields with validation MUST link to their error messages:

```tsx
<Input
  id="email"
  aria-describedby={errors.email ? "email-error" : undefined}
  aria-invalid={!!errors.email}
/>
{errors.email && (
  <p id="email-error">{errors.email.message}</p>
)}
```

- `aria-describedby` — points to the error message element
- `aria-invalid` — bound to the error state

## data-testid

All interactive elements MUST have a `data-testid` attribute for Playwright test stability:

### Naming convention

`data-testid` values use **kebab-case** with the pattern: `{context}-{element-type}`

| Element | data-testid example |
|---|---|
| Email input | `data-testid="login-email-input"` |
| Password input | `data-testid="login-password-input"` |
| Submit button | `data-testid="login-submit-button"` |
| Navigation link | `data-testid="sidebar-workshops-link"` |
| Table row | `data-testid="users-table-row"` |
| Modal | `data-testid="delete-confirmation-dialog"` |

### Rules

- Page-scoped context prefix to avoid collisions — `login-email-input` not just `email-input`
- Repeating elements use a suffix: `data-testid={`users-table-row-${user.id}`}`
- `data-testid` is for test stability only — never use it for styling or application logic
- Prefer semantic locators (`getByRole`, `getByLabel`) in tests; `getByTestId` is the fallback

## autoFocus

The first interactive field in every form MUST have `autoFocus` so keyboard users land on the right field without extra tab presses.

## Checklist for new interactive elements

When adding or modifying any interactive element, verify:

1. **Semantic HTML** — use the right element (`<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, `<select>`)
2. **Visible label** — `<Label htmlFor>` for form fields, descriptive text for buttons/links
3. **`aria-label`** — ONLY if no visible label or text content exists (icon-only buttons, toggles, landmarks)
4. **`aria-describedby`** + **`aria-invalid`** — for fields with validation errors
5. **`data-testid`** — unique, kebab-case, context-prefixed
6. **`autoFocus`** — on first field of every form
